print(int("1" + "2") * 3)
print(len("ab" * 3) + 1)
print(str(1 + 2) * 2)
```
hint: Just like in maths, `*` happens before `+`, even with strings.
hint: Line 2: the brackets of `int()` are worked out first, so `"1" + "2"` happens before the multiplication.
hint: Line 4: `1 + 2` is 3, `str(3)` is `"3"`, and a string times 2 repeats it.
right: Line 1 repeats `"2"` three times first (`"222"`), then joins: `1222`. Line 2 joins into `"12"`, converts to 12, and multiplies: `36`. Line 3: `"ababab"` has 6 characters, plus 1 is `7`. Line 4: `str(3)` is `"3"`, repeated twice: `33`.
wrong: Do `*` before `+`, and work out the inside of each function call first. `"2" * 3` is `"222"`, so line 1 is `1222`. `int("12") * 3` is 36. `len("ababab")` is 6, plus 1 is 7. `str(3) * 2` is `"33"`.
wrong[121212\n36\n7\n33]: `*` happens before `+`, so only `"2"` is repeated: `"1" + "222"` is `1222`.
wrong[1222\n9\n7\n33]: `"1" + "2"` joins two strings into `"12"`; it doesn't add 1 and 2. So line 2 is `12 * 3 = 36`.
wrong[1222\n36\n7\n6]: `str(1 + 2)` is the text `"3"`, and text times 2 repeats it: `33`.

